C(11082,'stated without proof','C','RS-DELZANT','R-Delzant1962 R-Springer1959 R-CCMT2014','The intended normalized Clifford invariant has not been identified from the supplied text. The naive full-Clifford difference is false: overR, q=〈−1,1,1,1〉 has w2=0 but C(q)−C(〈1,1,1,1〉) is the nontrivial Brauer class. A corrected Clifford-extension formula includes discriminant terms.','Delzant1962; precise modern formulaCCMT2014; raw interpretation refuted2026-11-19',proof='research/root_proofs.md R8; Clifford_real_rank4 certificate',confidence='medium',origin='Antoine Delzant offprint')
C(11083,'proof sketched','B','RS-DELZANT','R-Delzant1962 R-Stacks03PK','Naturality and Whitney additivity are valid for quadratic spaces under field extension; a forgotten tilde changes the target convention, not the resulting naturality theorem.','Delzant1962',origin='Antoine Delzant offprint')
C(11084,'proof sketched','B','RS-DELZANT','R-Delzant1962 R-EKM','Over number fields the local classification and signatures are needed in addition to low-degree cohomological invariants. OverR, rank together with all coefficients of(1+u)^r recovers the negative indexr; total Stiefel–Whitney class without rank does not recover dimension.','Classical quadratic-form classification; Delzant1962 formulation',proof='research/root_proofs.md R8; real_SW_binary_recovery certificate',origin='Antoine Delzant offprint')
C(11085,'open on the pages','B','RS-DELZANT','R-Delzant1962 R-Milnor1970 R-Merkurjev2006 R-Merkurjev1981','The degree2 injectivity question is answered positively for fields of characteristic≠2: Milnor’s s2 identifies K2M/2 with I²/I³, and Merkurjev’s norm-residue theorem identifies K2M/2 with H²(F,μ₂^⊗2). Neither identification alone is the whole answer. Merged with the1961 letter question.','Milnor1970; Merkurjev result1981, accessible proof2006; first prepublication dates not established',proof='research/root_proofs.md R8',origin='Antoine Delzant offprint')
C(11086,'open on the pages','B','RS-DELZANT','R-Delzant1962 R-Stacks03PK R-Merkurjev2006','The field Kummer sequence gives H²(F,μ₂)≅Br(F)[2]. Merkurjev’s theorem says every2-torsion Brauer class is a sum of quaternion symbols; this is the exact surjectivity sought. The assertion is not extended to arbitrary schemes without Pic/2.','Kummer formalism predates source; norm-residue surjectivity1981',origin='Antoine Delzant offprint')

# Unindexed cote55 claims are expanded below into the actual propositions, not a
# single generic catch-all record for each page.
def D(n,suffix,status,cls,fate,statement,proof=None,refs='R-EGAIV1',search='RS-DIMENSION',date='EGAIV first part1964; manuscript undated',confidence='high'):
    return C(n,status,cls,search,refs,fate,date,statement=statement,proof=proof,confidence=confidence,suffix=suffix)
D(11095,'','proof sketched','B','For compact Stein subsets under the appropriate CartanA/B and finite-presentation hypotheses, the equivalence is established. Noetherianness requires additional hypotheses: Siu’s criterion is exact, and the Buchweitz–Flenner convention explicitly includes semianalyticity.','For a compact analytic set with a Stein-neighborhood basis and the required coherent-sheaf generation/acyclicity, Γ and O⊗_A− give the finite-presentation equivalence; A=Γ(K,O). Compactness is used to replace local generation by finitely many global sections.',refs='R-Siu1969 R-BuchweitzFlenner2003 R-Benoist2026',search='RS-STEIN',date='Frisch1967; Siu received1968-07-12,published1969; modern formulation2003/2026',confidence='medium')
D(11096,'','false as stated','C','The abstract H¹-only LemmaA is false. X=RP² with constant sheafC is compact, O coherent with noetherian stalks, and H¹ of every finite-rank local system vanishes, but the nontrivial sign local system hasΓ=0. The separately pencilled quasi-compact-neighborhood-basis variant is not refuted by this example.','The main written hypotheses(quasi-compact X, coherentO, noetherian stalks, H¹(X,F)=0 for every coherentF) do not imply that coherent sheaves are equivalent to finitely presented modules overΓ(X,O).',proof='research/root_proofs.md R9; RP2_local_system_certificate',refs='R-BuchweitzFlenner2003 R-Benoist2026',search='RS-STEIN',date='Counterexample checked2026-11-19')
D(11097,'','open on the pages','B','Adding global finite generation for every coherent sheaf repairsβ. A finite global presentation and H¹-exactness identify the adjunction unit and counit; the analytic versions obtain generation from CartanA plus compactness.','Assume X is quasi-compact, O coherent, Γ exact on coherent sheaves, and every coherent sheaf is globally generated. Then every coherent sheaf admits a finite global presentation, Γ and sheafification give an equivalence with finitely presented A-modules.',proof='research/root_proofs.md R9',refs='R-BuchweitzFlenner2003 R-Benoist2026',search='RS-STEIN',date='Established analytic formulations2003/2026; direct proof2026-11-19')
D(11098,'a','stated without proof','C','Under the repaired finite-presentation equivalence, exactness of stalks gives flatness overA by the finite-relations criterion. The claim cannot be deduced from the falseH¹-only lemma.','Under the repaired equivalence of CL-SA18-11097, each stalkO_x is flat as anA-module.',proof='research/root_proofs.md R9',refs='R-BuchweitzFlenner2003',search='RS-STEIN',date='Conditional consequence checked2026-11-19')
D(11098,'b','stated without proof','C','Under the repaired equivalence, coherent kernels of finite free sheaf maps correspond to finitely generated kernels overA, proving coherence. No unconditional noetherianity follows.','Under the repaired equivalence of CL-SA18-11097, A=Γ(X,O) is a coherent ring.',proof='research/root_proofs.md R9',refs='R-BuchweitzFlenner2003 R-Ballico2005',search='RS-STEIN',date='Conditional consequence checked2026-11-19')
D(11099,'','stated without proof','F','The word pur and the unfinished maximal-ideal clause do not determine a precise notion. Searches in the analytic/coherent-ring comparators did not identify this exact statement.','The phrase about A being pur and its maximal ideals has insufficient surviving definitions to determine a theorem.',refs='R-Siu1969 R-BuchweitzFlenner2003 R-Benoist2026',search='RS-STEIN',date='Unidentifiable as of2026-11-19',confidence='low')
D(11100,'','open on the pages','B','The general question has a negative answer. LSY2012 Theorem4 gives the valuation-domain criterion: all finite polynomial-ideal contractions are finitely generated iff dimension≤1. The explicit coherent-product counterexample also disproves even the degree0 assertion. A monic polynomial in the ideal gives the positive Theorem3.','For an arbitrary coherent ringA and finitely generated idealJ⊂A[T], J∩A and bounded-degree parts need not be finitely generated. Explicitly A=∏_{n≥1}k[[u,v]], J=((u)_n−(v)_nT,(v^n)_n); its nth projected contraction is(u,v)^n, needingn+1 generators.',proof='research/root_proofs.md R11 (independently checked by agent46)',refs='R-LSY2012 R-Yengui2011 R-Stacks00O7 R-Stacks00NT',search='RS-COHERENCE',date='LSY received2010-05-04,online2011-02-16,printed2012; Yengui preprint2011-05-07')
D(11101,'a','open on the pages','C','Polynomial coherence is not automatic for coherent rings; the unrestricted proposal cannot be used to prove the analytic equivalence. Soublin is a bibliographic negative lead, explicitly unverified at original-text level; LSY gives exact positive bounded-degree statements under monicity.','The suggested generalization from coherentO to all polynomial algebras O[T1,…,Tr], with exact global sections on their finitely presented modules, is a stronger hypothesis/programme, not a proved consequence of LemmaA.',refs='R-LSY2012 R-Soublin1970 R-Benoist2026',search='RS-COHERENCE',date='Soublin1970 original not opened; LSY2011/2012; Benoist2026',confidence='medium')
D(11101,'b','proof sketched','B','Theα part follows from exact global sections and commuting with the filtered colimit of finite presentations under the stated quasi-compact/acyclic-neighborhood hypotheses. The source does not finishβ. Modern globally-finitely-presented formulations retain that limitation.','The natural map M→Γ(X,O⊗_A M) is an isomorphism in the stated finite-presentation setting; extending it to filtered unions requires the explicit quasi-compact neighborhood and cohomology-commutation assumptions.',refs='R-Benoist2026 R-BuchweitzFlenner2003',search='RS-STEIN',date='Modern formulation2026; undated draft')
D(11102,'','proof sketched','B','The unique-continuation condition forces ascending chains of coherent subobjects to stabilize globally once they stabilize locally; quasi-compactness provides a finite cover. For analytic compacta, noetherianity is governed by Siu’s exact component criterion, so the semianalytic application cannot be dropped.','LemmaB assumes quasi-compactX, coherentO with noetherian stalks, and the stated unique-continuation property for coherent ideals with integral quotients; it concludes noetherianness of coherent modules and then ofA when the repaired global equivalence holds.',refs='R-Siu1969 R-BuchweitzFlenner2003',search='RS-STEIN',date='Siu1968 received/1969publication',confidence='medium')
D(11103,'a','false as stated','A','The proof phrase that the maximal ideal of a noetherian local ring is generated by dimA elements is false. A=k[x,y]/(x,y)² localized at(x,y) has dimension0 and a nonzero2-generated maximal ideal. A system of parameters generates an m-primary ideal instead.','The source’s local-dimension inequality does not require regular local rings; its auxiliary claim m_A can be generated by dimA elements must be replaced by an m_A-primary parameter ideal.',proof='research/root_proofs.md R10',date='EGA dimension theory1964; proof repair2026-11-19')
D(11103,'b','proof sketched','A','The local dimension upper bound is valid and is proved using a system of parameters and the principal ideal theorem, not the false maximal-ideal generator assertion.','For a local homomorphism A→B of noetherian local rings, dimB≤dimA+dim(B/m_A B).',proof='research/root_proofs.md R10')
D(11103,'c','stated without proof','A','Under flatness the reverse inequality gives equality; this is the usual flat local dimension formula. The source explicitly adds flatness.','For a flat local homomorphism A→B of noetherian local rings, dimB=dimA+dim(B/m_A B).')
D(11103,'d','stated without proof','A','The generalized principal ideal theorem gives height at mostr for a prime minimal over anr-generated ideal; it does not say every prime containing the ideal has that height.','In a noetherian ring, if an ideal is generated byr elements, every prime minimal over it has height at mostr; the local geometric codimension consequence uses the stated irreducibility/minimality assumptions.')
D(11104,'a','proved there','A','Finite-type field dimension is transcendence degree on each irreducible component. The source’s pointwise dimension is the dimension of neighborhoods, not automatically the local-ring Krull dimension at a nonclosed point.','For an integral finite-type k-algebraA, dimA=trdeg_k Frac(A). For a finite-type k-scheme, local topological dimension atx is the maximum dimension of the irreducible components throughx.')
D(11104,'b','proof sketched','A','Field extension preserves the dimension of finite-type schemes; a purely formal assertion for arbitrary non-finite-type algebras is not extracted.','If X is finite type overk and K/k is any field extension, dimX_K=dimX, with the analogous component/pointwise statements under the indicated choice of point.')
D(11104,'c','proved there','A','The finite-type product dimension is the sum of dimensions; reducible schemes are handled componentwise.','For nonempty finite-type k-schemesX,Y, dim(X×_kY)=dimX+dimY.')
D(11105,'a','proved there','A','The closure of the image has dimension at most that of the source for finite-type morphisms over a field. The purely topological analogue is false, as the source separately demonstrates.','If f:X→Y is a morphism of finite-type k-schemes, dim closure(f(X))≤dimX.')
D(11105,'b','proof sketched','A','For quasi-finite f the dimensions ofX and its image closure agree componentwise; the hypothesis excludes positive-dimensional generic fibres.','If f:X→Y is quasi-finite between finite-type k-schemes, dimX=dim closure(f(X)).')
D(11105,'c','proved there','A','The actual source example is the generic-point open immersion SpecK→SpecB for a DVR B: its image closure has dimension1 although the source has dimension0. It is finite type because K=B[1/π]. Thus finite type of the morphism alone is insufficient without the finite-type-over-a-field setting.','For a DVR B with fraction fieldK, SpecK→SpecB is a finite-type morphism with dimSpecK=0 and dim closure(image)=1.',proof='research/root_proofs.md R10')
D(11106,'a','proved there','A','Associated primes commute with arbitrary direct sums in the stated locally noetherian quasi-coherent setting. An element has finite support; the prime annihilator of its finite tuple equals one of the component annihilators. The source’s arbitrary-family quantifier is retained.','For any family of quasi-coherent modulesF_i on a locally noetherian scheme, Ass(⊕_iF_i)=union_i Ass(F_i).')
D(11106,'b','proved there','A','The zero divisors on a finite module are exactly the union of its associated primes. This is a standard associated-point result in EGAIV§3.','For a finite moduleM over a noetherian ringA, a∈A is a zero divisor onM iff a belongs to some prime inAss(M).')
D(11106,'c','proved there','A','Finite prime avoidance turns the associated-prime criterion into the stated non-zero-divisor criterion for an ideal. Finiteness ofAss(M) is used.','For a finite moduleM over a noetherian ringA, an idealJ contains anM-regular element iff J is contained in no associated prime ofM.')
D(11106,'d','proof sketched','A','A noetherian scheme is reduced iff it is generically reduced and has no embedded associated points; both conditions are needed.','For a noetherian ringA, reducedness is equivalent to reduced localizations at its minimal primes together with Ass(A)=Min(A).')
D(11107,'a','proved there','A','EGA0IV14.1.4 proves exactly the subset inequality and finite-closed-cover maximum.','For any topological spaceX and subsetY with induced topology, dimY≤dimX. If X is a finite union of closed subsetsXi, dimX=max_i dimXi.')
D(11107,'b','proved there','A','EGA0IV14.1.5–14.1.7 supplies the local maximum, supremum and open/locally-finite-closed cover statements. The locally finite qualification is retained.','Define dim_xX=inf_{U neighborhoodx}dimU. Then dimX=sup_x dim_xX; local dimension is the maximum across a finite closed cover throughx; dimension is the supremum across an open cover or a locally finite closed cover.')
D(11107,'c','proved there','A','EGA0IV14.1.11 gives upper semicontinuity directly from the neighborhood definition.','The functionx↦dim_xX is upper semicontinuous for the topological dimension convention used on these pages.')
D(11107,'d','corrected by himself later (both places)','A','The cancelled continuous-image inequality at c55:11 is replaced on the same page by the two-point counterexample; EGA0IV14.1.12 prints that example.','The identity from the discrete two-point space to the Sierpinski two-point space is a continuous surjection increasing combinatorial dimension from0 to1.',proof='research/root_proofs.md R10')
D(11108,'a','proved there','A','EGA0IV14.1.8–14.1.10 proves the closed-point supremum and the zero-dimensional finite-discrete criterion, with the noetherianT0 hypothesis essential.','A nonempty noetherianT0 space has dimension0 iff it is finite discrete; its dimension is the supremum of local dimensions at closed points, and a point is isolated iff its local dimension is0.')
D(11108,'b','proved there','A','EGA0IV14.2.1–14.2.3 gives the exact codimension convention and inequalities; codimension of a reducible closed subset is an infimum over its components.','For closed nonemptyY⊂X, dimY+codim(Y,X)≤dimX. For closedY⊂Z⊂T, codim(Y,Z)+codim(Z,T)≤codim(Y,T). Passing to an open subset cannot decrease the stated global codimension.')
D(11108,'c','proved there','A','EGA0IV14.2.5–14.2.6 identifies finite-union codimension with the minimum, and local codimension with the minimum of components through the point whenY is locally noetherian.','Codimension of a finite closed union is the minimum of the individual codimensions. For locally noetherian closedY, x↦codim_x(Y,X) is lower semicontinuous.')
D(11109,'a','false as stated','A','The false equivalence appears verbatim in EGA0IV14.3.3(b). Pilloni–Stroh2014 XIV2.4.2 footnote records Gabber’s correction and Chen’s26September2005 letter: require each irreducible component to be equicodimensional. The explicit semilocal affine counterexample has all printed hypotheses and maximal chains of lengths1 and2.','A finite-dimensional noetherianT0 space that is equidimensional,equicodimensional and catenary need not have all maximal chains of the same length.',proof='research/root_proofs.md R10',refs='R-EGAIV1 R-PilloniStroh2014',date='False formulation1964; documented correction letter2005-09-26,publication2014')
D(11109,'b','proved there','A','EGA0IV14.3.2 correctly equates catenarity with codimension additivity along irreducible triples, assuming finite codimensions. It is unaffected by the later14.3.3 error.','If all codimensions between nested irreducible closed subsets are finite, equal lengths of saturated chains with fixed endpoints are equivalent to codim(Y,T)=codim(Y,Z)+codim(Z,T) for irreducibleY⊂Z⊂T.')
D(11109,'c','proof sketched','B','The strong equal-maximal-chain condition gives the dimension/codimension formulas and passes to closed irreducible subsets and unions of components. Use the strong condition, not the false weaker(b).','For a finite-dimensional noetherianT0 space whose maximal chains all have equal length, dimY+codim(Y,X)=dimX for nonempty closedY; irreducible closed subsets and unions of irreducible components remain biequidimensional.',refs='R-EGAIV1 R-PilloniStroh2014',date='EGA1964; corrected interpretation2014')
D(11110,'a','false as stated','B','The lattice literally transcribed is the product lattice: replacing e1+ik e2 by ik e2 leaves the same lattice. Hence the quotient is a product of two elliptic curves and is projective, contradicting the intended example.','For real irrationalk, the displayed lattice generated by e1,ie1,e2,e1+ik e2 gives (C/(Z+iZ))×(C/(Z+ikZ)); it does not give a nonprojective torus.',proof='research/root_proofs.md R10; torus_lattice certificate',refs='R-MilneAV',search='RS-TORUS',date='Literal counterexample verified2026-11-19')
D(11110,'b','open on the pages','B','The intended existence statement is true. The alternative lattice〈(1,0),(i,0),(0,1),(k,i)〉 with real irrationalk has an elliptic fibration and no complementary elliptic subtorus; Poincare complete reducibility excludes projectivity. This is explicitly a different example, not a correction of the ink.','There exists a nonprojective2-dimensional complex torus fitting into an extension of elliptic curves. The given alternative lattice realizes it.',proof='research/root_proofs.md R10 (independently checked by agent16)',refs='R-MilneAV',search='RS-TORUS',date='Classical complete reducibility; explicit verification2026-11-19')
D(11111,'','stated without proof','F','The terminal question about a reduced module stops before a definition or quantifiers are supplied. Associated-point sources explain neighboring assertions but do not identify this one.','The incomplete last clause concerning a reduced module cannot be reconstructed as a unique mathematical proposition.',date='Unidentifiable as of2026-11-19',confidence='low')

